{
  "image_name": "1e97cbbd338d665ed0f749258bbd80c6.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how cardiac or cellular fuel metabolism is remodeled across several connected pathways. It combines glucose, fatty acid, amino acid, ketone body, and branched-chain amino acid metabolism, with colored arrows indicating whether metabolites, transporters, or fluxes tend to increase or decrease in two disease states.\n\nOverall, the image suggests a shift away from efficient mitochondrial oxidative metabolism. In the glucose network, transport and glycolytic intermediates are altered, with changes around glycogen, pyruvate, lactate, and side pathways such as the pentose phosphate and hexosamine routes. Entry of pyruvate into mitochondria and its conversion by PDH appear reduced, while lactate handling changes at the membrane.\n\nFatty acid metabolism also appears extensively remodeled: uptake, activation to fatty acyl-CoA, transport through the carnitine shuttle, and β-oxidation are all affected, alongside accumulation or redistribution of lipid species such as DAG, TAG, ceramide, and acylcarnitines. Inside mitochondria, the Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation show multiple decreases, implying impaired energy production.\n\nThe lower panels indicate additional rewiring of ketone body use and BCAA metabolism, with altered βOHB transport and breakdown and disturbed BCAA/BCKA processing. As a whole, the figure depicts broad metabolic inflexibility centered on mitochondrial dysfunction.",
